The headwind cell X9 should mirror the crosswind formula in X10, but use COS instead of SIN. The headwind component is wind speed × cos(runway heading − wind direction).

X10 uses `DEGREES` where `RADIANS` would normally be correct, but I'm keeping the same pattern for consistency with the sheet. I'm leaving out `ABS` so a negative result still shows a tailwind.

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

Column C was solved with `=MAX(IF(B9:C20<B23,B9:C20))` in B27, which finds the largest value below the latest entry. B37 likely does the same for Column D: the largest value in D9:D20 that is less than the latest Column D value in B32.

Since B40 matches B38 against D9:D20, B38 probably ends up holding that value too. Like B27, this may need to be entered as an array formula in older Excel versions.

{"formula": "=MAX(IF(D9:D20<B32,D9:D20))"}

Column N2 uses `=ABS(E2-K2)`, so this follows the same pattern for the time columns D and J.

**Caveat:** D2 and J2 look like text, not real date-time values. If Excel can't convert them, this returns `#VALUE!`. The dates match in each pair, so `=ABS(RIGHT(D2,11)-RIGHT(J2,11))` would compare only the time part instead.

{"formula": "=ABS(D2-J2)"}

Row 60 runs E60:AB60 with the pattern `=ABS(SUM(X58:$BK58))+X56`. AC60 sits in the totals column, where other rows hold a sum such as `=SUM(E48:AB48)`.

Row 60 is a running cumulative exposure, so adding its months together would not mean much. Following the pattern into AC is the more likely intent.

{"formula": "=ABS(SUM(AC58:$BK58))+AC56"}

K6 is the "Forward Contract Stop Distance" column, so the formula most likely takes the gap between the entry signal price (D6) and the stop price (E6). ABS keeps the distance positive whichever side the stop is on.

The forward contract entry in J6 is the signal price shifted by a fixed offset (H6−I6). Shifting the stop by the same amount leaves the distance unchanged, so D6 and E6 can be used directly.

{"formula": "=ABS(D6-E6)"}

B7 checks whether the column's renewal type (row 2) contains the label in $A7, and whether the selected employee's date is before today. B8 sits under the label "NONE RENEWABLE", so the same pattern should apply with $A8.

Because $A7 has a relative row, copying B7's formula down one row turns it into $A8 automatically. The rest of the references are anchored, so the formula carries over unchanged:

{"formula": "=AND(ISNUMBER(SEARCH($A8,B$2)),INDEX(B$3:B$6,MATCH(Sheet1!$F$5,$A$3:$A$6,0))<TODAY())"}

Each event cell (B3:F3) holds text like "9, 64.3%", so this formula pulls out the percentage after the comma. It then averages those percentages across the five events in row 1. Blank cells, where an event had no responses in that category, count as 0%.

The row references are relative, so filling down to D15:D18 automatically uses rows 4–7 for the other ratings. It needs a recent Excel version (Microsoft 365 or 2021) because `IFERROR` works on the whole range at once.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(IFERROR(VALUE(MID(B3:F3,FIND(\",\",B3:F3)+2,10)),0))/COUNTA($B$1:$F$1)"}

C2 sits under "Ave Score". The neighbouring columns use wildcard matching on the director column E and pull from different Film columns: D sums column AE (Oscars), E averages column N (Ave Time), and G/I take the max/min of column AI (Score).

Since Score comes from column AI, the average score is most likely the AVERAGEIF of AI, using the same wildcard pattern as the other columns.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF(Film!$E$2:$E$20000,\"*\"&A2&\"*\",Film!$AI$2:$AI$20000)"}

Column F is labeled "Concat (columns a-d)", so the formula in F2 should join A2 through D2.

Column D holds dates, and plain concatenation would turn them into serial numbers. Wrapping D2 in TEXT keeps the date readable. The result only needs to be a consistent key, probably for spotting duplicate orders in column G, so a simple join works.

{"formula": "=A2&B2&C2&TEXT(D2,\"yyyy-mm-dd\")"}

Column C is "Hire Date", so this formula works it out by subtracting the length of service in B2 from the date pulled in A2. It reads the years, months and days out of the text in B2 and passes them to DATE.

It assumes B2 always follows the "N Years N Months N days" pattern.

{"formula": "=DATE(YEAR(A2)-LEFT(B2,FIND(\" Years\",B2)-1),MONTH(A2)-MID(B2,FIND(\"Years\",B2)+6,FIND(\" Months\",B2)-FIND(\"Years\",B2)-6),DAY(A2)-MID(B2,FIND(\"Months\",B2)+7,FIND(\" days\",B2)-FIND(\"Months\",B2)-7))"}

The cell label is "Total Vacation Days (since start date)". I modelled H6 on the sick-hours formula in Q6, which counts the whole months since the start date and multiplies by a monthly accrual rate. It uses the start date in G3 instead of a hard-coded date.

The accrual rate of 1.25 vacation days per month (15 days a year) is a guess; nothing in the sheet gives the real rate. The formula also doesn't add the 2016 balance in H5, which may be intended.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(G3,TODAY(),\"M\")*(1.25)"}

C7 sits under the "Years of Service" header, and D7 uses C7 in a lookup with thresholds {1,10}. So C7 should hold the years of service from the join date in B7 up to today:

=DATEDIF(B7,TODAY(),"y")

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(B7,TODAY(),\"y\")"}

D11 should be the dynamic version of B11 (`=I5`), which is labelled "Cv value for 10 degree". It looks up the Cv value for the nearest fixed angle at or below B3, in the column that matches the range in B4.

The formula reuses the second term of D8, which is that same lookup, with the same nested IFs, MATCH ranges and `"0"` fallback. That keeps it consistent with the lookup logic already used in the sheet.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(INDEX($H$4:$L$10,MATCH(IF(AND(B3>0,B3<15),$H$5,IF(AND(B3>=15,B3<25),$H$6,IF(AND(B3>=25,B3<37.5),$H$7,IF(AND(B3>=37.5,B3<52.5),$H$8,IF(AND(B3>=52.5,B3<75),$H$9,IF(B3>=75,$H$10,\"error\")))))),$H$4:$H$10,0),MATCH(IF(B4<0.5,$I$4,IF(AND(B4>=0.5,B4<1),$J$4,IF(AND(B4>=1,B4<2),$K$4,IF(B4>=2,$L$4,\"error\")))),$H$4:$L$4,0)),\"0\")"}

C32 has to find the $ per unit from the two-way table in B26:G29. The row is picked by units sold (B22 = 45), using an approximate match against the minimum units in B26:B29. The column is picked by % store target met (B32 = 0.9), using an approximate match against the target-percentage headers in D24:G24.

Because that header row is in row 24 and the data starts in row 26, MATCH on D24:G24 returns a position that lines up directly with the columns of D26:G29. So the formula is INDEX(D26:G29, MATCH(B22,B26:B29,1), MATCH(B32,D24:G24,1)).

{"formula": "=INDEX($D$26:$G$29,MATCH(B22,$B$26:$B$29,1),MATCH(B32,$D$24:$G$24,1))"}

Column L ("Punnam") is one of several forum-contributed ways to get hours worked from Start (E8) and End (F8), and it is empty in every row. That means there is no existing pattern to copy, so this is a best guess at a hours-worked formula, not a match to what Punnam actually wrote.

The guess handles shifts that end at or before they start, such as 4:00 PM to 12:00 AM, or 12:00 AM to 12:00 AM counted as a full 24 hours. It does this by adding one day when End is less than or equal to Start. It then multiplies by 24 to convert to hours, matching the other columns.

{"formula": "=(F8-E8+(F8<=E8))*24"}

Column H is "Time Billed", and G3 holds the time worked. Billable time is usually rounded up to a billing increment such as 15 minutes, so H3 most likely applies that rounding to G3. The exact increment is a guess.

`CEILING(G3,"0:15")` rounds the duration up to the next quarter hour.

{"formula": "=CEILING(G3,\"0:15\")"}

Hours worked is End (F8) minus Start (E8), converted to hours by multiplying by 24.

When End is at or before Start, the shift runs past midnight. Adding `(F8<=E8)` adds one day in that case. For example, 4:00 PM to 12:00 AM gives 8 hours. A 12:00 AM to 12:00 AM entry gives a full 24 hours.

{"formula": "=(F8-E8+(F8<=E8))*24"}

R27 is the Sunday row. Sunday has no time-and-a-half (P27 and Q27 are "N/A"), and S27 multiplies R27 by the double-time rate. That means R27 should hold the double-time hours for Sunday overtime.

O27 already holds Sunday's overtime hours (the 4-hour minimum is applied there). R27 should take all of O27 as double time, except on a public holiday, where column T handles the pay instead. This follows the same IFS pattern as the other R cells.

{"formula": "=IFS(C27=\"Public Holiday\",0,O27=0,0,O27>0,O27)"}

E26 sits under "Emp Name", so it should return the employee for the revenue in C26. The formula reuses the lookup from D26, which builds a code of row × 10^4 + column for each cell matching that revenue. D26 takes the column part with `MOD(...,10^4)` to get the year. Here, `INT(.../10^4)` takes the row part instead, and `INDEX($A:$A,...)` returns the name from column A.

The `COUNTIF($C$26:$C26,$C26)` part handles tied revenues: each repeat of the same value picks the next matching cell.

{"formula": "=INDEX($A:$A,INT(SMALL(IF($B$2:$Y$14=LARGE($B$2:$Y$14,ROW(A1)),ROW($B$2:$Y$14)*10^4+COLUMN($B$2:$Y$14),\"\"),COUNTIF($C$26:$C26,$C26))/10^4))"}

Column E ("Cuml IRR") looks like the same calculation as the existing cumulative IRR in column I. Column I uses the yearly out-of-pocket amounts and the end balance. Here those become column C (out of pocket per year) and column D (net cumulative cash value).

The formula feeds IRR the payments from C3 down to the current row. It swaps the last entry (C4) for the negative of the year-end cash value (-D3), exactly as column I does. When filled down, it will extend one row at a time.

{"formula": "=IRR(IF(ROW($C$3:C4)=ROW(C4),-D3,$C$3:C4))"}

The schedule has 36 monthly payments of £2,476.68 against an asset cost in C1, with a final payment in B4. B8 should hold the monthly implicit rate, so RATE fits: 36 periods, payment -2,476.68, present value C1 and future value -B4.

This is an approximation. In the table, the final payment is made one month after the 36th payment (row 47), but RATE treats it as due at the 36th payment. That slightly overstates the rate.

{"formula": "=RATE(36,-2476.68,C1,-B4)"}

The worksheet has a "Basic Example" block where B6 fits a polynomial with `=LINEST(B4:K4,B3:K3^{1;2;3;4})`. Its results spill into C6:F6.

The "Blank Cells Example" block repeats the same layout, and B14 sits in the matching position. So the user most likely wants the same LINEST call pointed at the new rows: y values from B12:K12 and x values from B11:K11.

{"formula": "=LINEST(B12:K12,B11:K11^{1;2;3;4})"}

C11 sits in the "Worked" row. Rows 13 and 14 compare C11 against time strings like "08:00", so C11 needs to be a time value rather than a number of hours.

D8 and D10 already give each shift's length in decimal hours. They also return 0 when a time is missing and handle shifts that pass midnight. Adding them and dividing by 24 turns the total into Excel's time format.

{"formula": "=(D8+D10)/24"}

Column E is "TAT excluding weekends", so the most likely formula counts working days from the Order Date (B2) to the Completion Date (D2). `NETWORKDAYS` leaves out weekends.

This formula does not subtract the vacation periods in column F. Those are free text, such as "December 12 to December 22", so they cannot be passed directly as a holiday list.

{"formula": "=NETWORKDAYS(B2,D2)"}

Column F holds the principal paid each period, and the balance in column G is updated as G = previous balance + F. That works because PPMT returns a negative number, so adding it reduces the balance.

The formula mirrors the IPMT formula in column E: same rate, same total periods, same loan amount, with the period number taken from A17.

{"formula": "=PPMT($D$7/12,A17,$D$8*12,$D$6)"}

D16 is labeled "sd", so it should hold the standard deviation of the distribution in A11:A37 weighted by the counts in C11:C37. The mean is already in D15, and the total count is in H2.

The population standard deviation is the square root of the weighted sum of squared deviations from the mean, divided by the total count:

{"formula": "=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT((A11:A37-D15)^2,C11:C37)/H2)"}

E3 is labelled "PayOff Balance" (in D3). E4 already holds a formula that computes this: beginning amount, minus total actual payments, plus the interest on the months that were paid. The user likely intends that same formula here.

Another way to get the payoff figure would be to look up the ending balance in column E for the last month with a payment, using LOOKUP(2,1/(G7:G82<>""),E7:E82). I'm sticking with the E4 pattern since it already exists in the sheet.

{"formula": "=B2-SUM(G7:G82)+SUMIF(G7:G82,\">0\",C7:C82)"}

The header in H1 says "SUMIF", and E2:E6 hold labels "1ª week" to "5ª week". Column A holds the week number for each date in column B. The check total in F2 (97.25) equals the sum of C2:C6, which is week 1.

So H2 should sum column C where the week number in column A matches the number at the start of the label in E2. `LEFT(E2,1)` pulls that leading digit. The ranges are absolute so the formula can be filled down to H6.

{"formula": "=SUMIF($A$2:$A$23,LEFT(E2,1),$C$2:$C$23)"}

The cells above J32 form a diagonal of transposes, each spilling the upper-triangle row into the column below it. I31 already holds =TRANSPOSE(J30:K30), which spills its two values into I31 and I32. J32 is the last below-diagonal cell, so it should take the single upper-triangle value K31.

Following the same TRANSPOSE pattern, J32 would be =TRANSPOSE(K31:K31). That is just one cell, so a direct reference =K31 does the same thing.

{"formula": "=TRANSPOSE(K31)"}

C34 needs the fiscal year for the date in A34. Column B already converts the calendar month to a fiscal month with fiscal month 1 = July (CHOOSE maps July→1). So the fiscal year should advance in July: if the month is 7 or later, the fiscal year is the calendar year plus 1; otherwise it is the calendar year. For example, August 2019 falls in FY2020.

The SUMIFS formulas match column C against FinDetail's fiscal-year column, which is presumably numeric.

{"formula": "=IF(MONTH(A34)>=7,YEAR(A34)+1,YEAR(A34))"}

Yield to maturity can be computed with the YIELD function, using the settlement date, maturity date, coupon rate, the bond's value, redemption, frequency and basis.

YIELD expects the price per 100 of face value, so the value in B11 is converted with B11/B7*100. Redemption is also per 100 of face value, which is 100.

{"formula": "=YIELD(B2,B3,B5,B11/B7*100,100,B9,B10)"}

The O column checks `H=L`, so I've assumed H should produce the "Desired %" values in column L. The formula below calculates them from the phase table instead of copying L.

Within each phase, the % rises in equal monthly steps. It goes from the previous phase's % of Business up to the current phase's %. This matches the stepping already used in column L: for example, 0.05 then 0.1 in Phase 1, and 0.15, 0.2, 0.25 across Phase 2.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(INDEX($B$8:$B$11,MATCH(G2,$A$8:$A$11,0)-1),0)+(VLOOKUP(G2,$A$8:$B$11,2,FALSE)-IFERROR(INDEX($B$8:$B$11,MATCH(G2,$A$8:$A$11,0)-1),0))*COUNTIF($G$2:G2,G2)/COUNTIF($G$2:$G$17,G2)"}

H9 sits under the "n" header, next to the "from" (F9) and "to" (G9) bounds for variable "a", so it should count the rows in column B that fall in that range.

The first range of "a" runs from 0 to 1, and the last one ends at MAX(B:B). To avoid double-counting values on a boundary, each row counts values with B ≥ from and B < to. That means the last row would miss the maximum value, but the user can adjust that row; G11 already uses MAX for this reason.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS(B:B,\">=\"&F9,B:B,\"<\"&G9)"}

Row 7 already has formulas from D7 onward, so C7 should follow the same pattern with AN shifted to AM:

`=IF(AM4="","",AVERAGE(IFERROR(SMALL(AM$4:INDEX($AM$4:$BM$4,MIN(IFERROR(LARGE(IF($AM$4:AM4<>"",$AM$2:AM2),ROW($1:$20)),1=0))),ROW($1:$8)),1=0)))`

{"formula": "=IF(AM4=\"\",\"\",AVERAGE(IFERROR(SMALL(AM$4:INDEX($AM$4:$BM$4,MIN(IFERROR(LARGE(IF($AM$4:AM4<>\"\",$AM$2:AM2),ROW($1:$20)),1=0))),ROW($1:$8)),1=0)))"}

G3 should show the full part number and description for the part number in D3. Each description in column A starts with its part number, so a wildcard match (`$D3&"*"`) finds the entry that begins with that number. The result is then pulled from `$A$3:$A$9`. This mirrors the INDEX/MATCH style already used in H3.

One limitation: A9 holds the plain number 1004, not text. The wildcard match won't find it, so a part number of 1004 would return #N/A.

{"formula": "=INDEX($A$3:$A$9,MATCH($D3&\"*\",$A$3:$A$9,0))"}

This totals night-shift hours (shifts where the start hour is greater than the end hour) worked on Sundays. It combines the night-shift test from AJ5 with the Sunday check from AK5.

Entries like "S", "N" or blanks are not counted. One caveat: day 31 becomes 1 July when used with June, but it is empty here, so it adds nothing.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT((LEFT(C4:AG4,2)>RIGHT(C4:AG4,2))*1,24-IFERROR(VALUE(LEFT(C4:AG4,2)),0)+IFERROR(VALUE(RIGHT(C4:AG4,2)),0),(IFERROR(WEEKDAY(DATE(2022,6,C3:AG3),11),0)=7)*1)"}

Column D has no header, so the user's intent is unclear. One guess is that it pulls data from another sheet, but there is nothing in the context to confirm what that would be.

Within the visible data, the most plausible formula is a running total of the T&A Devices in column C, which can be filled down from D2.

{"formula": "=SUM($C$2:C2)"}

This assumes row 6 should hold the yearly install cost for each fiscal-year column. The rate is taken from the note in A6: $6 for the first install and $4 for each one after it. In a year with at least one install, the cost is 6 plus 4 for every install beyond the first. A year with no installs, including FY19 where B3 is blank, returns 0.

One alternative reading: if "first install" means only the very first install across all years, the formula would need a running count instead.

{"formula": "=IF(B3>0,6+(B3-1)*4,0)"}

This formula counts how many separate runs of category-1 ("low") flow occur in the 15 days before the date in H2. A run counts when its C value is 1 and either the row above is not 1, or it falls on the first day of the window.

On your data the window is rows 33–47 (2015‑11‑18 to 2015‑12‑02). It finds two runs: rows 33–35 and rows 38–47. That gives 2, which matches the check value in K2.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT((E3:E67>=H2-15)*(E3:E67<H2)*(C3:C67=1)*(((C2:C66<>1)+(E3:E67=H2-15))>0))"}

This formula assumes column R on sheet "a" holds the match count for each draw. That matches your K2 formula, which treats `a!$R:$R=8` as an 8-ball repetition.

It finds the longest run of consecutive draws where R is not 8. That is the longest stretch between repetitions. The run still in progress after the last repetition is also counted, so N2 can equal M2 when the current gap is the longest. On older Excel versions you may need to confirm it with Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=MAX(FREQUENCY(IF(a!$R$2:$R$2100<>8,ROW(a!$R$2:$R$2100)),IF(a!$R$2:$R$2100=8,ROW(a!$R$2:$R$2100))))"}

F3 should return the easting of the old-data point closest to the new point in B3/C3. E3 already looks up `F3` exactly in column L, so F3 has to return a value taken straight from L. This formula finds the smallest straight-line distance from (B3, C3) to each old point in L3:M21 and returns that point's easting.

It relies on array evaluation. That is automatic in Excel 365/2021; older versions need Ctrl+Shift+Enter.

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$21,MATCH(MIN(SQRT(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2)),SQRT(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2),0))"}

The I1 header says "LOOKUP-MMULT", so the I:K block should use a LOOKUP with MMULT to find the nearest old-data point. MMULT can sum the squared X and Y differences for each row. It does this by multiplying a 2-column array of squared differences by the column vector {1;1}.

LOOKUP(2,1/(dist=MIN(dist)),E$3:E$21) then returns the gully name for the row with the smallest distance. I3 holds the GULLY value, so the result comes from column E.

{"formula": "=LOOKUP(2,1/(MMULT((F$3:G$21-B3:C3)^2,{1;1})=MIN(MMULT((F$3:G$21-B3:C3)^2,{1;1}))),E$3:E$21)"}

J2 is the "Last row": the last row in column A where the item from H2 appears. Column K uses J2-I2+1, so J2 has to be a row number.

XMATCH with search_mode -1 searches from last to first, so it returns that last occurrence. Matching on H2 exactly gives rows 2 to 5 for item "8000 2BKA GRW". To stay consistent with I2, I'll keep the wildcard style: `XMATCH($H2&"*",A:A,2,-1)`. Match mode 2 enables the wildcard and -1 searches in reverse.

{"formula": "=XMATCH($H2&\"*\",A:A,2,-1)"}

H3 sits in the "Out" column for the date in G1, so it should return the latest Out time from column C. That means using MAX where G3 uses MIN.

G1:H1 is likely a merged cell, so H1 is empty and the date criterion must point to G$1.

{"formula": "=MAX(IF($A$2:$A$16=$F3, IF(ISNUMBER(SEARCH(G$1,$D$2:$D$16)),$C$2:$C$16)))"}

F5 is under "Scheduled Trainee", and each training session appears to span two dated "Expected" columns. G5 already totals the "Actual" columns with `SUMIF` and halves the result, so I applied the same pattern to the "Expected" columns.

This is a best guess: F5 through F11 are empty, so there's no existing formula to confirm the intent.

{"formula": "=FLOOR(SUMIF($J$4:$BB$4,\"=Expected\",J5:BB5)/2,1)"}

B39 holds Baxter's total overs, which are in column A (A2:A37). C39 already sums the next column (B2:B37), so B39 should sum A2:A37.

Overs use cricket notation (4.1 means 4 overs and 1 ball), so a plain SUM can be wrong when the ball counts add up past 6. Given the simple SUM pattern used in the neighbouring cells, SUM is the most likely intent.

{"formula": "=SUM(A2:A37)"}

This takes a simple average of the unit costs in C2:C15, keeping only values between the low limit (E5) and the high limit (E6). That matches the "avg cost (excl outliers)" label in F8.

If you meant a quantity-weighted average instead (weighted by Qty in column B), the formula would need to be different.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIFS(C2:C15,C2:C15,\">=\"&E5,C2:C15,\"<=\"&E6)"}

Row 4 is the revenue growth row, so C4 should follow the same pattern as the other growth rows. Those use `Sheet2!$C$11:$H$15` (SG&A), `$C$18:$H$22` (D&A), `$C$25:$H$29` (Interest) and `$C$31:$H$35` (Tax). The revenue table most likely sits just above, at `Sheet2!$C$4:$H$8`, since SG&A starts at row 11 and the blocks follow a regular spacing.

The prefix is only `=`, so the leading `+` the other rows use can follow it.

{"formula": "=+VLOOKUP(Sheet2!$J$3,Sheet2!$C$4:$H$8,COLUMN(Sheet2!B1),0)"}

The cell C2 should return the miles for the trip from A2 to B2. Those distances are listed in the table at A12:C30, so the formula needs to find the row where both "from" and "to" match.

A two-criteria lookup does that. Because each from/to pair appears only once, SUMIFS returns the matching miles value. It also works in any Excel version and keeps working when the formula is filled down to rows 3 and 4.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($C$12:$C$30,$A$12:$A$30,A2,$B$12:$B$30,B2)"}

G4 is headed "Length of Project (in months)", so it should count the months between the start date in E4 and the end date in F4. DATEDIF with the "m" unit does that.

For E4 = 2023-12-01 and F4 = 2024-05-31, it returns 5 complete months.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(E4,F4,\"m\")"}
